one after another :  27.3s   9,219 tokens
all at once       :   5.7s   9,203 tokens

4.8x faster on the wall clock, 1.00x the tokens

This is the multi-agent win, and it is worth being precise about what kind of
win it is. Nothing got cheaper. The same six briefings cost the same tokens
and made the same model calls; they simply waited at the same time instead of
in a queue.

6 workers gave 4.8x rather than 6x. The missing part is the
work your own machine does — retrieval, the warehouse, JSON — which does
not overlap with itself as neatly as waiting on a network does.

The condition for this pattern is strict and easy to violate: the six tasks
must not need each other. The moment quarter two's briefing depends on what
quarter one found, you are back in a queue with extra machinery.
